---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';
import CommentsSection from '../../components/CommentsSection.vue';
import gitguardianPoc from '../../assets/gitguardian_poc.jpg';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang as any);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Dear Diary, I Enabled GitGuardian on My Repositories: Is It Actually Worth It? 🌸",
    date: "October 24, 2026",
    subtitle: "A developer diary entry by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "After seeing GitHub and Git security recommendations everywhere, I decided to activate GitGuardian across my repositories.",
    introDesc: "As a Full-Stack developer managing client projects, APIs, and databases, I had to ask myself: is this just another noisy notification bot, or a critical safety net that every programmer should turn on? Here is everything I learned, how it works, and whether it's truly worth activating.",
    bannerLabel: "Official GitGuardian Platform & Documentation:",
    bannerUrl: "https://www.gitguardian.com",
    screenshotTitle: "Local Defense: ggshield Blocking a Leaked Secret Before Push",
    screenshotCaption: "Live terminal view of ggshield pre-commit hook catching a leaked Stripe API key in a local .env file and blocking the commit before the secret ever leaves localhost.",
    sections: [
      {
        heading: "1. The Context: Why I Decided to Enable GitGuardian",
        paragraphs: [
          "Lately, I kept seeing prompts and recommendations across GitHub highlighting automated secret scanning and integrations with **GitGuardian**. If you've been coding for any length of time, you know the dread: one late-night commit, a rushed `git add .`, an accidental stash pop, and suddenly an API key or database credential is baked right into your repository history.",
          "As a Full-Stack developer juggling multiple client projects, e-commerce integrations, payment gateways, and cloud databases, human error is always a lurking reality. I decided to turn GitGuardian on across my GitHub repositories to see what the experience was really like."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "2. What is GitGuardian and What is Its Real Purpose?",
        paragraphs: [
          "At its core, **GitGuardian** is an automated secrets detection and DevSecOps security engine designed specifically to protect credentials throughout the software development lifecycle.",
          "Its primary purpose is to scan your code for **over 400+ types of sensitive secrets**: API keys (OpenAI, Stripe, Google Cloud, AWS), database connection strings with embedded passwords, private SSH keys, JWT secrets, OAuth tokens, and client credentials.",
          "Why is this so critical? Because automated malicious crawlers scan public GitHub commits **within seconds of push**. If a valid API token leaks into public view, it can be harvested, abused, and rack up thousands of dollars in cloud bills or compromise customer databases before you even realize you made a mistake."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "3. How Does GitGuardian Work? (Real-Time Push & ggshield)",
        paragraphs: [
          "GitGuardian approaches secret detection through three defensive layers:",
          "**1. Real-Time Post-Push Scanning:** The moment you push code to GitHub (public or private), GitGuardian's engine inspects the commit diff in seconds. If an exposed secret is detected, you get an immediate high-priority alert via email or dashboard with the exact file and line number.",
          "**2. Deep Historic Repository Crawling:** When you first connect a repository, GitGuardian doesn't just look at the `main` branch HEAD. It crawls every commit, every past merge, and every forgotten branch in the repository's entire history to find legacy secrets that might have been committed years ago.",
          "**3. Local Pre-Commit Protection (ggshield):** This is where it gets truly powerful. Through its CLI tool `ggshield`, you can configure a Git pre-commit hook on your machine. It scans staged files **locally** and cancels the `git commit` command if a secret is found, preventing the credential from ever touching GitHub in the first place."
        ],
        codeLabel: "Installing and Configuring ggshield as a Local Git Hook",
        code: `# 1. Install ggshield CLI via pip or package manager
pip install ggshield

# 2. Authenticate with your GitGuardian account
ggshield auth login

# 3. Install ggshield as a pre-commit hook in your local repository
ggshield install --mode=local

# Now, any commit containing an API key or .env secret will be BLOCKED automatically:
# Status: [ALERT] Incident detected -> Commit cancelled!`
      },
      {
        heading: "4. The Honest Verdict: Is It Actually Useful or Just Noise?",
        paragraphs: [
          "After testing and using it, **yes, it is overwhelmingly useful—especially for Full-Stack developers and freelance teams**.",
          "Here are the genuine pros and the realistic caveats you should keep in mind:",
          "**• The Good:** It provides immense peace of mind. GitGuardian includes automated **validity checks**, meaning it tests whether an exposed key is actually active and live or just a dummy placeholder. This drastically cuts down on false alarms.",
          "**• The Reality Check:** Activating GitGuardian does not replace good security hygiene. If an alert fires, you cannot simply do `git push --force` or delete the line from your code. **Once a secret is committed to Git, consider it permanently compromised.** The only safe response is to immediately rotate and revoke the credential in your service dashboard.",
          "**• Test Fixtures:** If your automated tests use realistic-looking mock tokens, you may occasionally get an alert. Fortunately, GitGuardian allows you to mark test files as ignored directly in `.gitguardian.yaml`."
        ]
      },
      {
        heading: "5. What I Learned as a Full-Stack Developer: Beyond .gitignore",
        paragraphs: [
          "One of the biggest lessons this reinforced for me is that **`.gitignore` is not a security system**.",
          "Many developers believe that adding `.env` to `.gitignore` makes them completely safe. But `.gitignore` only ignores *untracked* files. If someone on your team accidentally added `.env` before adding the gitignore rule, or if a branch merge pulls in tracked configuration files, Git will continue to track and upload those secrets silently.",
          "Enabling GitGuardian pushed me to establish stricter protocols: using `.env.example` templates, injecting credentials strictly via runtime environment variables in production, and having `ggshield` standing guard as an automated checkpoint on every commit."
        ]
